.TH IMAGE-SPEED-METER 1 "November 2026" "image-speed-meter 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
image-speed-meter \- measure how fast an image (or any file) downloads
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B image-speed-meter
[\fIoptions\fR]
\fIurl\fR [\fIurl\fR ...]
.br
.B image-speed-meter
[\fIoptions\fR]
\(aq\fIjson-array-of-urls\fR\(aq
.br
.B image-speed-meter
[\fIoptions\fR]
.B \-\-file
\fIpath\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B image-speed-meter
makes
.I N
(10 by default) sequential requests to every given URL.
Each request starts only after the previous response has been received
completely.
For every URL it prints the time and size of each request, then the average
request time, the total amount of data downloaded and the download speed.
.PP
URLs can be passed as separate arguments, as one JSON array, or in a file.
They are measured one after another; a failure on one URL does not stop the
others.
.PP
Requests are made with
.BR curl (1).
In
.B auto
mode, a URL that curl cannot get is retried in headless Chromium
(see
.BR FALLBACK ).
The last downloaded body is saved to disk unless
.B \-\-no\-save
is given.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-runs " \fIN\fR"
Number of sequential requests per URL. Default: 10.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mode " \fImode\fR"
How requests are made:
.RS
.TP 9
.B auto
curl first, headless browser if curl is blocked (default).
.TP
.B curl
curl only; a browser is never started.
.TP
.B browser
headless browser only.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " \fIpath\fR"
Read URLs from \fIpath\fR, one per line.
Empty lines are ignored, and \(aq#\(aq starts a comment.
URLs from the file are added after the ones on the command line.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-out " \fIdir\fR"
Directory to save the downloaded file in. It is created if missing.
Default: \fI./downloads\fR.
The file name comes from the last part of the URL path; an extension is
added from the Content-Type if the name has none.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-save
Do not save the downloaded file.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-timeout " \fIsec\fR"
Timeout for a single request, in seconds. Default: 60.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the results as a JSON array, one object per URL, instead of text.
No progress lines are printed.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print only the per-URL summary, not every request.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help text and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH OUTPUT
For every request: its number, the method used, its time, its size and its
speed.
For every URL, a summary:
.TP 12
.B method
\fBcurl\fR, \fBbrowser\fR, or \fBcurl + browser\fR if the method changed
during the runs.
.TP
.B requests
number of completed requests.
.TP
.B avg time
mean request time, in seconds (s).
.TP
.B total data
sum of all response bodies, in megabytes (1 MB = 1\|000\|000 bytes), with
the exact byte count in parentheses.
.TP
.B speed
total bytes divided by the total time of all requests, in MB/s and in
Mbit/s (1 Mbit = 1\|000\|000 bits).
.PP
With curl, time and size are taken from curl itself
(\fItime_total\fR and \fIsize_download\fR), so process start-up is not
counted.
With the browser, time is measured from navigation start until the whole
body is available; browser start-up is not counted.
Caching is disabled in the browser, so every run hits the network.
.SH FALLBACK
In
.B auto
mode, curl is replaced with headless Chromium for the rest of the runs of a
URL when:
.IP \(bu 2
the response status is 401, 403, 429 or 503;
.IP \(bu
a 2xx response is an HTML page (usually an anti-bot interstitial);
.IP \(bu
curl itself fails (connection reset, TLS error and so on).
.PP
The request that triggered the fallback is not counted.
If the browser gets an anti-bot challenge that runs scripts and then
redirects, it waits for the redirect, then repeats that run so the measured
time does not include the challenge.
Chromium is started once and reused for all URLs.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CHROME_PATH
Path to a Chromium or Chrome executable.
If unset, the first of
.BR chromium-browser ,
.BR chromium ,
.BR google-chrome-stable ,
.BR google-chrome ,
.BR chrome
and
.B microsoft-edge
found in
.B PATH
is used, then the usual macOS and Windows install locations.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every URL was measured successfully.
.TP
.B 1
At least one URL failed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line arguments.
.SH EXAMPLES
Measure one image with the default 10 requests:
.PP
.RS
.nf
image-speed-meter https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3f/Fronalpstock_big.jpg
.fi
.RE
.PP
Five requests, browser only, without saving:
.PP
.RS
.nf
image-speed-meter \-n 5 \-\-mode browser \-\-no\-save https://example.com/photo.jpg
.fi
.RE
.PP
Several URLs as a JSON array, JSON output:
.PP
.RS
.nf
image-speed-meter \-\-json \(aq["https://a.example/1.jpg","https://b.example/2.png"]\(aq
.fi
.RE
.PP
URLs from a file, summaries only:
.PP
.RS
.nf
image-speed-meter \-q \-f urls.txt
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR curl (1),
.BR chromium (1)
